Z||000000||84Bill||04-22-2001||10:44 PM||||I have an interesting problem and need an opinion. I was reassembling the hand brakes and noticed that the L/h caliper puck will not move when the hand brake is pulled and now I have a really spongy brake. AND since the friggin bleeders snapped off (friggin apes overtightend them) I can't bleed the rear claipers. anywho my question is, is the caliper bad?<P>SOAPBOX<BR>&gt; <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Why is it that ppl think it it neccessary to tighten stuff up so much that it will never come off again in one piece. I rounded off 2 lugnuts on the left rear then the bleed valves snapped. I mean good grief, the mechanics now days are apes all of them. I spun off the cradle boltes without a single problem and when I put them back in I just snugged them up and a 1/8th turn. SHEESH!!! guess if I was a mechanic I should have used a 15mm impact socket and a 3 foot 1/2 drive breaker bar and tightend it a bit more! 6 or 7 turn should do it!!! AHHHHHHHHHH!!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||04-22-2001||11:14 PM||||Fiero AL calipers often develope electrolytic corosion like things on a boat would. It effectively welds the parts. Read my cave notes on it. The odds of them breaking in steel calipers are about 50/50 to start with. In Fiero that means they more often break than open.<P><BR>yes, basically the caliper is toast. Autozone has the best price on rebuilt that I've seen.<P>when you get the new ones... remove the bleeders and coat them lightly with anti sieze. also find/make caps for them.<P>one of the big bleeder killers is stuff getting down the bleeder opening and corroding the thing from the inside.<P>My favorite caps come off various GM products front calipers. You can usually find a few Grand Am's etc w/o front wheels on in the scrap yard.<P>any rubber/plastic cap that fits snug will help.||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||04-23-2001||07:47 AM||||Hummmm ok maybe not ALL mechanics are apes. I shall consult the cave for more info. <BR>Thanks||||reg||14
